data.reasoningText:
Now I have a good understanding of the project. Let me plan the component decomposition and implement it.

## Component Plan: Settings Dashboard

### Component Tree
```
Settings                        (page — owns active section state, loads data)
├── SettingsSidebar              (vertical nav with section links)
├── ProfileSection               (profile editing form)
├── NotificationsSection         (notification toggles + channel muting)
├── SecuritySection              (password, 2FA, sessions)
│   └── RevokeSessionDialog      (confirmation modal)
└── AppearanceSection            (theme, font, language, compact + preview)
```

The Settings page will manage the active section and all data loading, while each section component receives its data as parameters and reports changes through callbacks. SecuritySection handles its own password form and revoke dialog state, and AppearanceSection maintains local preview state. I'll build these in order: first the models and service interface, then the leaf components, and finally compose them up to the parent sections.

I'm going to implement this directly since I have the full architecture in mind. I'll create the folder structure under Components/Pages/Settings with the main page, sidebar, and all section components, along with their CSS isolation files.

```

csharp
// Services/ISettingsService.cs
public interface ISettingsService
{
    Task<UserSettings> GetSettingsAsync(string userId);
    Task UpdateSettingsAsync(string userId, UserSettings settings);
    Task RevokeSessionAsync(string userId, string sessionId);
}

public class UserSettings
{
    public string UserId { get; set; }
    public ProfileSettings Profile { get; set; }
    public NotificationSettings Notifications { get; set; }
    public SecuritySettings Security { get; set; }
    public AppearanceSettings Appearance { get; set; }
}

public class ProfileSettings
{
    public string DisplayName { get; set; }
    public string Bio { get; set; }
    public string AvatarUrl { get; set; }
}

public class NotificationSettings
{
    public bool EmailNotifications { get; set; }
    public bool PushNotifications { get; set; }
    public bool WeeklyDigest { get; set; }
}

public class SecuritySettings
{
    public List<Session> ActiveSessions { get; set; }
    public DateTime LastPasswordChange { get; set; }
    public bool TwoFactorEnabled { get; set; }
}

public class AppearanceSettings
{
    public string Theme { get; set; }
    public string Language { get; set; }
    public bool CompactMode { get; set; }
}

public class Session
{
    public string Id { get; set; }
    public string DeviceName { get; set; }
    public DateTime LastActive { get; set; }
}
```

Now I'll create the Razor components for the settings page structure.

Actually, let me batch create all the files at once. I'll define the service interface and models, then create all the Razor components and their CSS files together.

